\title{The Cantorian Hierarchy of Infinities: An Explicit Tower, Hartogs Bounds, and the Continuum}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We present a self-contained development of the core structural facts governing the sizes of infinite sets, organized around the slogan that ``infinity is a place you can visit.'' Working within the standard axioms of set theory (Zermelo--Fraenkel with the Axiom of Choice, ZFC), we (i) construct an explicit strictly increasing tower of infinite cardinals $\aleph_0 < 2^{\aleph_0} < 2^{2^{\aleph_0}} < \cdots$, a truncated \emph{beth} sequence; (ii) prove that the cardinals form a proper class with no maximal element, via Cantor's theorem in power-set form; (iii) establish Hartogs' theorem, providing a well-orderable ordinal exceeding any given set \emph{without} invoking its power set; (iv) locate the Continuum Hypothesis (CH) precisely as the single inequality $\c \le \aleph_1$, given the ZFC theorem $\aleph_1 \le \c$; (v) apply K\"onig's theorem to show the continuum has uncountable cofinality and to prove the unconditional ZFC result $\c \ne \aleph_\omega$; and (vi) analyze $\aleph_0$ as the prototype of an ``unreachable'' cardinal --- regular and a strong limit, failing inaccessibility only through the uncountability clause. We discuss the independence of CH and directions toward large-cardinal theory.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The discovery that infinity comes in different sizes is among the most consequential in the history of mathematics. Georg Cantor's demonstration that the real numbers cannot be enumerated opened a hierarchy of transfinite magnitudes that has never been seen to terminate. This paper assembles the load-bearing facts of that hierarchy, stating each result inline with a proof sketch, and emphasizes a unifying theme: the \emph{ascent} through the infinities, treating each infinity as a place and the theorems as descriptions of how to travel between places, which cannot be reached from below, and which questions are beyond the reach of our axioms.

All results below are theorems of ZFC unless explicitly flagged as independent. For a set $S$, $|S|$ is its cardinality; $\aleph_0$ is the cardinality of the naturals, $\aleph_1$ the first uncountable cardinal, $\aleph_\omega$ the first cardinal of countable limit type, and $\c = 2^{\aleph_0}$ the cardinality of the continuum. $\mathcal{P}(S)$ denotes the power set.

\section{Preliminaries}

A \emph{cardinal} measures the size of a set: two sets have the same cardinality iff there is a bijection between them. Under the Axiom of Choice the cardinals are linearly ordered. For cardinals $c,d$, the power $d^c$ is the cardinality of the set of functions from a size-$c$ set to a size-$d$ set; in particular a subset of $S$ is an indicator function to $\{0,1\}$, so
\[
|\mathcal{P}(S)| = 2^{|S|}.
\]
An \emph{ordinal} is the order type of a well-ordering. The \emph{cofinality} $\cof(\lambda)$ of a limit cardinal $\lambda$ is the least length of a strictly increasing sequence with supremum $\lambda$; $\lambda$ is \emph{regular} if $\cof(\lambda)=\lambda$ and \emph{singular} otherwise.

\section{An explicit tower of infinities}

\begin{definition}[Cantor tower / truncated beth sequence]
Define $T:\mathbb{N}\to\Card$ by $T_0 = \aleph_0$ and $T_{n+1} = 2^{T_n}$. Thus $T_1 = 2^{\aleph_0} = \c$, $T_2 = 2^{2^{\aleph_0}}$, etc. This is the beth sequence $\beth_n$ restricted to finite indices.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Cantor]\label{thm:cantor}
For every cardinal $c$, $\;c < 2^c$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The map $x\mapsto\{x\}$ injects $S$ into $\mathcal{P}(S)$, so $|S|\le 2^{|S|}$. If $f:S\to\mathcal{P}(S)$ were onto, the diagonal set $D=\{x\in S: x\notin f(x)\}$ would satisfy $D=f(y)$ for some $y$, whence $y\in D \iff y\notin D$, a contradiction. Hence $|S| < |\mathcal{P}(S)| = 2^{|S|}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Every stage is infinite]
For all $n$, $\aleph_0 \le T_n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction: the base is equality, and $\aleph_0 \le T_n \le 2^{T_n} = T_{n+1}$ using Theorem~\ref{thm:cantor}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict monotonicity]
The sequence $(T_n)$ is strictly increasing: $T_n < T_{n+1}$ for all $n$, hence $T_m < T_n$ whenever $m<n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$T_n < 2^{T_n} = T_{n+1}$ is Theorem~\ref{thm:cantor}; a natural-number sequence strictly increasing at each successor step is strictly increasing throughout.
\end{proof}

This is the precise sense in which one can keep visiting larger infinities: an explicit itinerary $\aleph_0 < \c < 2^{\c} < \cdots$.

\section{No largest infinity}

\begin{theorem}[Power-set form]
For any set $S$, $|S| < |\mathcal{P}(S)|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:cantor} and $|\mathcal{P}(S)| = 2^{|S|}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Successor cardinals exist]
For every cardinal $c$ there is a cardinal $d$ with $c<d$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $d=2^c$ and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:cantor}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unboundedness / proper class]
There is no cardinal $M$ with $c\le M$ for all cardinals $c$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Such $M$ would give $2^M \le M$, contradicting $M < 2^M$. Hence the cardinals form a proper class.
\end{proof}

\section{Hartogs' theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Hartogs]
For every set $\alpha$ there is an ordinal $o$ with $|\alpha| < |o|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $o$ to be the initial ordinal of the cardinal successor $|\alpha|^+$, so $|o| = |\alpha|^+ > |\alpha|$. The successor exists because the well-orderable order types injecting into $\alpha$ form a set; the argument never forms $\mathcal{P}(\alpha)$, and $o$ is well-orderable by construction.
\end{proof}

The witnessing $o$ is the \emph{Hartogs number} of $\alpha$; iterating from $\aleph_0$ generates the aleph hierarchy $\aleph_0,\aleph_1,\aleph_2,\dots$.

\section{$\aleph_1$, the continuum, and CH}

\begin{theorem}[$\aleph_1$ uncountable]
$\aleph_0 < \aleph_1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\aleph_1$ is the cardinal successor of $\aleph_0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Easy half of CH]\label{thm:easy}
$\aleph_1 \le \c$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\c = 2^{\aleph_0}$ is uncountable, and $\aleph_1$ is the least uncountable cardinal.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Continuum Hypothesis]
CH is the statement $\aleph_1 = \c$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[CH is one inequality]
CH holds iff $\c \le \aleph_1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\aleph_1 = \c$ then $\c \le \aleph_1$. Conversely $\c \le \aleph_1$ with Theorem~\ref{thm:easy} and antisymmetry gives $\aleph_1 = \c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential form]
CH holds iff $\aleph_1 = 2^{\aleph_0}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Immediate from $\c = 2^{\aleph_0}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
G\"odel (1940, via $L$) and Cohen (1963, via forcing) showed $\Con(\mathrm{ZFC})\Rightarrow\Con(\mathrm{ZFC}\pm\mathrm{CH})$, so CH is independent of ZFC. We state and analyze it without asserting or denying it.
\end{remark}

\section{K\"onig's theorem and the continuum}

\begin{theorem}[Uncountable cofinality of the continuum]\label{thm:cofc}
$\aleph_0 < \cof(\c)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
K\"onig's theorem gives $\cof(\mu^\kappa) > \kappa$ for infinite $\kappa$ and $\mu\ge2$. With $\mu=2$, $\kappa=\aleph_0$: $\cof(2^{\aleph_0}) > \aleph_0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cofinality of $\aleph_\omega$]\label{thm:cofw}
$\cof(\aleph_\omega) = \aleph_0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\aleph_\omega = \sup_n \aleph_n$ is the limit of the countable increasing sequence $(\aleph_n)$, and no shorter sequence reaches a limit cardinal.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\c \ne \aleph_\omega$, unconditional]
$\c \ne \aleph_\omega$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Equal cardinals have equal cofinalities, but $\cof(\c) > \aleph_0 = \cof(\aleph_\omega)$ by Theorems~\ref{thm:cofc}, \ref{thm:cofw}.
\end{proof}

\section{$\aleph_0$ as the prototype of an unreachable cardinal}

\begin{definition}[Inaccessible]
$\kappa$ is \emph{inaccessible} if it is (1) uncountable, (2) regular, and (3) a strong limit ($x<\kappa \Rightarrow 2^x < \kappa$).
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[$\aleph_0$ regular]
$\aleph_0 \le \cof(\aleph_0)$, i.e. $\cof(\aleph_0)=\aleph_0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A finite union of finite sets is finite, so no finite increasing sequence has supremum $\aleph_0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\aleph_0$ strong limit]
For every $x<\aleph_0$, $2^x < \aleph_0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$x$ finite $\Rightarrow 2^x$ finite.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Inaccessible except for uncountability]
$\aleph_0$ is regular and a strong limit, yet is not inaccessible, failing only the uncountability clause.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Regularity and strong-limitness are the previous two theorems; inaccessibility would force $\aleph_0 < \aleph_0$. Thus the sole obstruction is the uncountability requirement, imposed by definition.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion and future directions}

Cantor's theorem is the engine of ascent and forbids a maximal infinity; Hartogs' theorem builds well-ordered larger infinities without power sets, generating the aleph scale. The clash of $2^{\aleph_0}$ and $\aleph_1$ is the site of CH, whose undecidable content compresses to one inequality. K\"onig's theorem constrains even that undecidable magnitude: $\c$ has uncountable cofinality, so $\c \ne \aleph_\omega$. Finally $\aleph_0$ mirrors, in miniature, the notion of inaccessibility.

Future directions include: the full beth function $\beth:\Ord\to\Card$ and its fixed points; further K\"onig consequences such as $\cof(2^\kappa) > \kappa$ and $\kappa < \kappa^{\cof\kappa}$; a model theory of set theory (forcing and inner models) sufficient to prove the independence of CH; and large cardinals beyond inaccessibility (measurable, Ramsey, supercompact) with their consistency-strength hierarchy.

